    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> declared independence on 16 February 1918 and, having lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> its interwar capital.</li><li>Occupied and annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1940, it was the first Soviet republic to declare independence, in 1990.</li><li>Restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004 and the eurozone in 2015.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> won the European basketball championships of 1937 and 1939, beginning a national passion for the sport; the 1939 tournament was held in a new hall built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

